WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
"What Are the Health
Benefits of Sorghum?"

00:00:11.235 --> 00:00:15.069
Sorghum is “The Forgotten Grain.”

00:00:15.135 --> 00:00:18.060
The United States is actually
the #1 producer of sorghum,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.720
“but it is typically not used to
produce food for American consumers.”

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.020
Instead it’s produced
mainly for feeding livestock,

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.080
or as pet food, or
even building materials.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.500
But it’s actually eaten as a
staple in other parts of the world,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.540
such as Asia and Africa where
it’s been eaten for thousands of years,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.420
making it currently the
fifth most popular grain grown

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.320
after wheat, corn, rice, and barley,
beating out oats and rye.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.340
Because sorghum is gluten-free,
because it can be deﬁnitively

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.620
considered safe for
people with celiac disease,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.240
we’re starting to see it emerge
as actual human food in the US,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.680
so I decided to look into
just how healthy it might be.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.140
Protein-wise it’s
comparable to other grains.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.020
But since when do we have to
worry about getting enough protein?

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.200
Fiber is what Americans
are desperately deficient in,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.140
and sorghum does pull
towards the front of the pack.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.280
The micronutrient composition
is relatively unremarkable;

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.860
here’s how it rates
on minerals, for example.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.240
Where sorghum shines is
on polyphenol content.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.960
Polyphenols are plant compounds
associated with reduced risk

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.660
of a number of chronic
diseases, including cancer,

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.200
cardiovascular diseases,
neurodegenerative disorders,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.160
and even all-cause mortality.
And if you compare different grains,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.580
sorghum really does pull
ahead, helping to explain

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.300
why its antioxidant
power is so much higher.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.040
Now, sorghum gets its
grainy little butt kicked

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.900
when it comes to fruits and vegetables,

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.260
but compared to other grains,
a sorghum-based

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.780
breakfast cereal, for example,
might have like eight times

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.300
the antioxidants than a
whole wheat-based cereal.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.620
But, what we care about is not
antioxidant activity in a test-tube,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.380
but antioxidant activity within our body.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.500
If you measure the antioxidant
capacity of your blood

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.500
after eating regular
pasta, it goes up a little.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.500
If you replace 30% of the wheat
flour with sorghum flour

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.300
it doesn’t go up much more.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.700
But if you eat 30% red sorghum
flour pasta, the antioxidant capacity

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.640
in your bloodstream
shoots up like 15-fold.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.460
See, there are multiple
types of sorghum.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.760
There’s black sorghum,
white sorghum, and red sorghum.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.820
This is how they look in grain form

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.220
(there’s evidently a yellow sorghum too).

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.600
And red sorghum, and
especially black, have like legit

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.000
fruit-and-vegetable-level
antioxidant activity.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.620
The problem is I can’t
find any of the colored ones.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.580
I can go online and buy red
or black rice, purple, blue,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.240
or red popping corn, and
purple or black barley,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.020
but I can’t find red or black
sorghum. Hopefully someday.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:02.280
But you can find white sorghum
for about four bucks a pound.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.000
Does it have any unique
health-promoting attributes?

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.840
It’s promoted as “…
An Underutilized Cereal Whole Grain

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.140
with the Potential to Assist in
the Prevention of Chronic Disease.”

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.120
But, what is the effect of sorghum
consumption on health outcomes?

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.500
An epidemiological study in China
found lower esophageal cancer

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.920
mortality rates in areas that
ate more millet and sorghum,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.360
compared to corn and wheat,
but that may have been more due

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.100
to avoiding a contaminating fungus

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.860
than from benefit in the sorghum
itself. Though, it’s possible.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.880
Just as “oats are the only
source of avenanthramides,”

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.880
which give oats some
unique health benefits,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.780
sorghum, even white sorghum,
contain unique pigments

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.740
known as 3-deoxyanthocyanins,
which are strong inducers

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.080
of some of the detoxifying
enzymes in our liver

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.540
and can inhibit the
growth of human cancer cells

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.300
growing in a petri dish
compared to red cabbage,

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.060
which has just regular
anthocyanin pigments.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:05.100
But, note that white sorghum didn’t
do much worse than red or black,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.600
which have way more of
the unique 3-deoxyanthocyanins;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.720
so, maybe it’s just
a general sorghum effect.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.100
You don’t know...until
you put it to the test.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.580
Sorghum was found to suppress
tumor growth and metastasis

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:25.920
in human breast cancer xenografts.
What does that mean?

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.760
The researchers conclude
that sorghum could be used as

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.480
“an inexpensive natural cancer
therapy, without any side effects…

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.600
strongly recommend[ing]
[the use of sorghum] as an edible

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.840
therapeutic agent…possess[ing]
tumor suppression…

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.840
and anti-metastatic effects
on human breast cancer.”

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.180
But xenograft means human breast
cancer implanted in a mouse.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:53.400
Yes, the human tumors grew slower
in the mice fed sorghum extracts

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.560
and blocked metastasis to the lung,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.580
and did the same for human colon
cancer, that again, was in mice;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.720
which can’t necessarily be
translated to how human cancers

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.240
would grow in humans,
since, for example,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.600
not only do these mice not
have a human immune system,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.480
they hardly have any immune system.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.760
They’re bred without a thymus gland,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.000
which is where cancer-fighting
immunity largely originates.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.300
I mean, how else could you
keep the mouse’s immune system

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.280
from rejecting the
human tissue outright?

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.240
But, this immunosuppression makes
these kinds of mouse models

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.300
that much more artificial,
and that much more difficult

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.160
to extrapolate to humans.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.500
And that’s a lot of what we
see in the sorghum literature—

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.000
in vitro data like in test tubes
and petri dishes, and rats and mice.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.440
There had just been this
critical missing piece of the puzzle

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.100
needed to link laboratory
data to actual beneﬁts in humans.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:51.180
Missing, that is, until...now.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.240
Thankfully we now have human
interventional studies

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.000
which we’ll explore next.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.840
Despite playing a significant role in
Africa and Asia as a staple grain,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.680
sorghum has only recently emerged
as a potential food source in the US.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.380
And it's not just a principal grain
in many parts of the world,

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.260
but evidently used in
folk medicine traditions.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.900
What might its health benefits be?

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.920
There has been some in vitro data
from test tubes and Petri dishes,

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.480
and "in vivo" data, meaning "within
the living" in laboratory animals,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.380
but only recently have we
started seeing human trials.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:33.600
In one study, subjects were asked to eat
sorghum pancakes versus corn pancakes

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.180
for supper for three weeks, and both
groups saw huge 20 to 30% drops

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.320
in their cholesterol, but they were
also all told to not eat eggs and other

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.520
cholesterol-boosting foods, so that
may very well have been playing a role.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.900
Another study tried biscuits.
Those eating sorghum biscuits

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.080
said they felt more satiated
than eating wheat biscuits,

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:59.640
but this didn't translate to differences
in intake at the subsequent

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.760
all-you-can-eat meal, so who cares
what they subjectively felt,

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.340
if it didn't cause them to eat any less.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:09.540
It's no wonder then
when you put it to the test,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.260
those eating sorghum versus wheat
biscuits didn't lose any weight,

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.560
though the data s a bit mixed.
A recent study concluded

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.500
that sorghum can be an
important strategy for weight loss

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:23.460
in humans, though the sorghum group
didn't actually lose more weight.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.100
But they appeared to be eating
hundreds more calories a day,

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.780
yet lost more body fat,

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.260
perhaps because of their greater
fiber consumption or other goodies

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:35.340
like resistant starch in the sorghum.
The vehicle they used though,

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.820
an artificially flavored, colored,
and sweetened mixture of water,

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:43.680
powdered milk, and either sorghum
or wheat may be good research-wise

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.860
so you can make a blinded control,
but it leaves you wondering

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.920
what would happen if you
actually ate the whole food?

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:55.260
The resistant starch is exciting though.
Most of the starch in sorghum

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.620
is either slow-starch or
fully resistant to digestion

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:03.000
in the small intestine, which offers
a banquet bounty of prebiotics

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.580
for your good gut flora
down in your colon.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.160
It's not evidently the sorghum starch
itself, but interactions with

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.060
the proteins and other compounds
that effectively act as starch blockers,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.240
inhibiting your starch-munching enzymes.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:23.100
Sorghum, then, ends up with the lowest
starch digestibility among grains,

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.300
which is why traditionally it was
considered an inferior grain,

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:31.440
but inferior in the sense of
not providing as many calories.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.140
But not providing as many calories is a
good thing in the age of epidemic obesity.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.220
Give someone a whole wheat muffin,
compared to a sorghum muffin

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:45.540
containing the same amount of starch,
and see significantly higher blood sugars

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:49.860
45 minutes to two hours later,
and a higher insulin spike

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:55.260
starting almost immediately. Overall,
a 25% lower blood sugar response,

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:59.730
and the body only had to release
a fraction of insulin to deal with it

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:00.660
less than half!

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:05.160
Same thing with diabetics: lower blood
sugar spike with a sorghum porridge

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:10.200
compared to grits that the body can
deal with, with a fraction of the insulin.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.340
So we need to educate people
how healthy sorghum is

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:18.240
and develop convenient
tasty products?

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.360
No, it's already convenient
and tasty just the way it is.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.320
One button press on my electric
pressure cooker with two parts water,

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.840
one part sorghum, and
it's done in 20 minutes.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.040
You can make one big batch and use
it all week just like you would rice.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:38.040
But where's the money in people eating
the intact, whole grain? Instead the

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:42.300
industry is looking at sorghum for its
enormous potential for exploitation

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:47.520
into so-called functional foods and food
additives or--- I mean, did you know

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.560
adding sorghum to pork or turkey patties
can decrease their cardboardy flavor?

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.200
And hey, why just eat it when you can
instead use it to make gluten-free beer?

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.480
It s funny: in HOW NOT TO DIET, when
I was talking about taxpayer subsidies

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.020
to the sugar or corn syrup, oil,
and livestock industries,

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.040
subsidizing cheap animal feed
to help make Dollar Menu meat,

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.780
I jokingly asked, When's the last time
you sat down to some sorghum?

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:21.300
But hey, now that I know how good it is for
you, maybe we should be taking advantage

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:24.240
of the quarter billion we're
spending to prop up the industry

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.080
and sit down to some
sorghum after all.

